WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.930
When a self-checkout isn't sure it has recognised an item,

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:06.530
it puts a prompt on the screen.

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:08.920
The shopper can accept it, or override it.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:16.350
Retailers treat a shopper's stated confidence in that prompt as a guide to how they'll actually use it at the till.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:24.250
A research poster from the School of Emergent Priorities asks whether stated trust moves the override hand at all.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:28.950
We asked shoppers six questions about how far they trusted the prompt.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:35.040
Then we set each shopper's answers beside the till's own log of their overrides.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.070
Two hundred and thirty-eight shoppers took part across four store sites,

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:42.200
and their override logs ran for twelve weeks.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.160
At low, medium and high trust alike,

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:49.830
the override rate held between twenty-six and thirty-one per cent.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:53.460
First-time and frequent shoppers followed the same flat pattern.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.600
We modelled every override against the shopper's trust score.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:02.776
The score explained no meaningful variance in override behaviour.

00:01:02.876 --> 00:01:06.306
Stated trust and the override hand move independently.

00:01:06.606 --> 00:01:08.916
Confidence in the prompt is not confidence in the till.

00:01:09.916 --> 00:01:14.026
The trust score passed every check we ran on the survey itself.

00:01:14.326 --> 00:01:15.796
It just doesn't reach the till.

00:01:16.796 --> 00:01:21.766
We built an instrument to measure something a shopper already does with their hand every week.

00:01:22.066 --> 00:01:24.866
It turns out the two were never talking to each other.
